{"parentUuid":"61ed29a3-3ee3-41c9-9e43-94b3a2c83029","isSidechain":false,"promptId":"6f58d9f8-96db-45c8-911a-d0087d4ebcee","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01N7ge4MmnmFj5bQpGV8ncmD","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t// ADR-001 Phase D — runtime client.\n2\t//\n3\t// Single browser-side fetch helper for talking to the Benny FastAPI runtime\n4\t// through the shell's /api/runtime/<path> proxy. All Prime-Silo widgets and\n5\t// agent-composed layouts go through here so:\n6\t//\n7\t//   • Agent-scope header injection has exactly one chokepoint.\n8\t//   • Tests can swap the underlying fetch by overriding window.fetch.\n9\t//   • Phase F (.aamp.view signing) gets a stable seam to verify before\n10\t//     pinned layouts replay against the runtime.\n11\t//\n12\t// Public API\n13\t//   runtimeFetch(path, init)        — request the runtime; auto-injects the\n14\t//                                     active agent scope when one is set via\n15\t//                                     withAgentScope() or createAgentRuntimeClient\n16\t//   fetchAsAgent(path, init, opts)  — explicit agent request; injects\n17\t//                                     X-Benny-Agent-Scope unconditionally\n18\t//   readRuntimeJson(response)       — parse the runtime's JSON body\n19\t//   listWidgets()                   — convenience wrapper around GET /widgets\n20\t//\n21\t// Phase D3 additions\n22\t//   saveView(workspace, filename, content, options?)  — POST a JSON layout\n23\t//                                                       into agent_sandbox/views\n24\t//   loadView(workspace, filename, options?)           — GET + parse a saved view\n25\t//   listViews(workspace, options?)                    — GET the saved-view names\n26\t//\n27\t//   Bound clients gain the same three methods with the agent scope baked in.\n28\t//\n29\t// Phase F additions\n30\t//   signView(view, options?)                — POST /views/sign; returns\n31\t//                                              {signature, canonical_payload}.\n32\t//                                              Human-only: AgentScopeMiddleware\n33\t//                                              rejects agent-scoped POSTs to\n34\t//                                              /api/views/* with 403, so a bound\n35\t//                                              agent client calling this\n36\t//                                              surfaces the security boundary\n37\t//                                              as a RuntimeError(status=403).\n38\t//   verifyView(view, signature, options?)   — POST /views/verify; returns the\n39\t//                                              boolean result of the runtime's\n40\t//                                              constant-time HMAC compare.\n41\t//\n42\t// Phase F2 additions\n43\t//   pinView(workspace, sourceFilename, opts) — POST /views/pin; promotes an\n44\t//                                              agent draft from\n45\t//                                              agent_sandbox/views/<src> to\n46\t//                                              the canonical workspace\n47\t//                                              views/<dst>. The runtime\n48\t//                                              re-reads, signs, embeds the\n49\t//                                              signature inline, and emits\n50\t//                                              VIEW_PINNED. Human-only by\n51\t//                                              middleware.\n52\t//\n53\t// Phase F2b additions\n54\t//   loadPinnedView(workspace, filename, opts) — GET /views/load/<ws>/<fname>;\n55\t//                                              reads a pinned view AND\n56\t//                                              verifies its embedded\n57\t//                                              signature in one round-trip.\n58\t//                                              Returns\n59\t//                                              {view, signature, valid, …}.\n60\t//                                              Reads are open to agents too\n61\t//                                              — pinned views are\n62\t//                                              deterministic-zone artefacts\n63\t//                                              and replay must succeed\n64\t//                                              under any caller. The\n65\t//                                              runtime is the sole holder\n66\t//                                              of BENNY_HMAC_KEY; the\n67\t//                                              browser only consumes\n68\t//                                              `valid`.\n69\t//\n70\t// Phase D2 additions\n71\t//   createAgentRuntimeClient(scope) — bound client whose every call auto-injects\n72\t//                                     the agent scope. Hand this to skills /\n73\t//                                     widgets via options.runtimeClient and the\n74\t//                                     X-Benny-Agent-Scope header is on every\n75\t//                                     /api/runtime/* hop, audited at the\n76\t//                                     AgentScopeMiddleware in Benny.\n77\t//   withAgentScope(scope, fn)       — run fn() with scope set as the active\n78\t//                                     agent scope; restores on return.\n79\t//                                     Synchronous code path inside fn() —\n80\t//                                     including the *first* awaited runtimeFetch —\n81\t//                                     picks up the scope. Awaiting through\n82\t//                                     other tasks loses context (no\n83\t//                                     AsyncLocalStorage in browsers); for\n84\t//                                     long-running agent loops use\n85\t//                                     createAgentRuntimeClient instead.\n86\t//   getActiveAgentScope()           — returns the currently active scope or null.\n87\t//\n88\t// Path semantics: callers pass paths *relative to the runtime API root*.\n89\t//   listWidgets()                  → /api/runtime/widgets\n90\t//   runtimeFetch(\"/agent_sandbox/health\") → /api/runtime/agent_sandbox/health\n91\t//\n92\t// Errors are thrown with .status set when the runtime returns non-2xx.\n93\t\n94\tconst RUNTIME_PROXY_PREFIX = \"/api/runtime\";\n95\t\n96\tconst VALID_AGENT_SCOPES = new Set([\"sandbox\", \"read_only\"]);\n97\t\n98\tconst AGENT_SCOPE_HEADER = \"X-Benny-Agent-Scope\";\n99\t\n100\t// Module-scoped active agent scope. withAgentScope() pushes/pops; bound\n101\t// clients read it via the call site. The unbound runtimeFetch auto-injects\n102\t// this onto outgoing requests when no header was set explicitly.\n103\tlet _activeAgentScope = null;\n104\t\n105\tfunction joinPath(prefix, path) {\n106\t  if (!path || path === \"/\") {\n107\t    return prefix;\n108\t  }\n109\t  return path.startsWith(\"/\") ? `${prefix}${path}` : `${prefix}/${path}`;\n110\t}\n111\t\n112\tasync function readErrorDetail(response) {\n113\t  try {\n114\t    const body = await response.text();\n115\t    if (!body) {\n116\t      return null;\n117\t    }\n118\t    try {\n119\t      return JSON.parse(body);\n120\t    } catch {\n121\t      return { detail: body };\n122\t    }\n123\t  } catch {\n124\t    return null;\n125\t  }\n126\t}\n127\t\n128\tfunction createRuntimeError(response, body) {\n129\t  const detail =\n130\t    (body && (body.detail || body.error)) ||\n131\t    `Runtime ${response.status} ${response.statusText || \"\"}`.trim();\n132\t  const error = new Error(detail);\n133\t  error.name = \"RuntimeError\";\n134\t  error.status = response.status;\n135\t  error.body = body;\n136\t  return error;\n137\t}\n138\t\n139\tfunction validateScope(scope) {\n140\t  if (!VALID_AGENT_SCOPES.has(scope)) {\n141\t    throw new Error(`Invalid agent scope: ${scope}. Expected one of ${[...VALID_AGENT_SCOPES].join(\", \")}.`);\n142\t  }\n143\t}\n144\t\n145\t/**\n146\t * Make a request to the Benny runtime through the shell proxy.\n147\t *\n148\t * Without an active agent scope this is a *human-driven* request and the\n149\t * runtime gates it via regular RBAC. When called from inside a\n150\t * `withAgentScope()` block — or via a client returned from\n151\t * `createAgentRuntimeClient(scope)` — the active scope is auto-injected as\n152\t * the `X-Benny-Agent-Scope` header so the runtime's `AgentScopeMiddleware`\n153\t * enforces the ADR-001 boundary on this hop.\n154\t *\n155\t * If the caller already set the header on `init.headers`, it wins — explicit\n156\t * intent beats ambient context.\n157\t */\n158\texport async function runtimeFetch(path, init = {}) {\n159\t  const url = joinPath(RUNTIME_PROXY_PREFIX, path);\n160\t  const headers = new Headers(init.headers || {});\n161\t\n162\t  if (_activeAgentScope && !headers.has(AGENT_SCOPE_HEADER)) {\n163\t    headers.set(AGENT_SCOPE_HEADER, _activeAgentScope);\n164\t  }\n165\t\n166\t  const response = await fetch(url, {\n167\t    ...init,\n168\t    headers,\n169\t    credentials: init.credentials || \"same-origin\"\n170\t  });\n171\t\n172\t  if (!response.ok) {\n173\t    const body = await readErrorDetail(response);\n174\t    throw createRuntimeError(response, body);\n175\t  }\n176\t\n177\t  return response;\n178\t}\n179\t\n180\t/**\n181\t * Same as runtimeFetch, but injects the X-Benny-Agent-Scope header\n182\t * unconditionally. Default scope is \"sandbox\" (writes confined to\n183\t * agent_sandbox/). Pass {scope:\"read_only\"} to disallow writes entirely.\n184\t *\n185\t * Agent-authored writes that target anything outside /api/runtime/agent_sandbox/\n186\t * will be rejected by the runtime with HTTP 403.\n187\t */\n188\texport async function fetchAsAgent(path, init = {}, options = {}) {\n189\t  const scope = options.scope || \"sandbox\";\n190\t  validateScope(scope);\n191\t\n192\t  const headers = new Headers(init.headers || {});\n193\t  headers.set(AGENT_SCOPE_HEADER, scope);\n194\t\n195\t  return runtimeFetch(path, { ...init, headers });\n196\t}\n197\t\n198\texport async function readRuntimeJson(response) {\n199\t  const text = await response.text();\n200\t  return text ? JSON.parse(text) : null;\n201\t}\n202\t\n203\t/**\n204\t * Convenience: GET /api/runtime/widgets and parse the JSON body. Throws\n205\t * RuntimeError if the runtime is unreachable or returns non-2xx.\n206\t *\n207\t * When called from an agent context (withAgentScope or\n208\t * createAgentRuntimeClient), the listing fetch is itself tagged with the\n209\t * agent scope header so the audit log records who asked.\n210\t */\n211\texport async function listWidgets() {\n212\t  const response = await runtimeFetch(\"/widgets\");\n213\t  return readRuntimeJson(response);\n214\t}\n215\t\n216\t/**\n217\t * Phase D3 — save a JSON layout view into the workspace's\n218\t * `agent_sandbox/views/` subtree.\n219\t *\n220\t * The runtime endpoint validates that the body parses as JSON and forces\n221\t * `subdir=\"views\"` so a stray call cannot silently land in `drafts/` or\n222\t * `notes/`. Callers may pass either a JSON string or any JSON-serialisable\n223\t * object — objects are stringified before send so the JSON validation on\n224\t * both sides agrees.\n225\t *\n226\t * Filename convention: `<id>.aamp.view`, but enforcement happens server-side\n227\t * (no path separators, no leading dot). This helper does not invent the\n228\t * extension — Phase F .aamp.view signing belongs at the manifest layer, not\n229\t * here.\n230\t *\n231\t * Returns the runtime's `SandboxWriteResponse`:\n232\t *   { status: \"written\", workspace, relative_path, bytes_written }\n233\t *\n234\t * Auto-injects the active agent scope when one is set; the bound-client\n235\t * variant forces it. Without scope this is a human-driven write — the runtime\n236\t * still validates JSON and path containment.\n237\t *\n238\t * @param {string} workspace\n239\t * @param {string} filename\n240\t * @param {string | object} content\n241\t * @param {{ agentId?: string }} [options]\n242\t * @returns {Promise<{status: \"written\", workspace: string, relative_path: string, bytes_written: number}>}\n243\t */\n244\texport async function saveView(workspace, filename, content, options = {}) {\n245\t  const body = serialiseViewContent(content);\n246\t  const payload = {\n247\t    workspace,\n248\t    subdir: \"views\",\n249\t    filename,\n250\t    content: body,\n251\t    agent_id: options.agentId || \"anonymous_agent\"\n252\t  };\n253\t  const response = await runtimeFetch(\"/agent_sandbox/views/save\", {\n254\t    method: \"POST\",\n255\t    headers: { \"Content-Type\": \"application/json\" },\n256\t    body: JSON.stringify(payload)\n257\t  });\n258\t  return readRuntimeJson(response);\n259\t}\n260\t\n261\t/**\n262\t * Phase D3 — load a saved view from `agent_sandbox/views/`.\n263\t *\n264\t * Returns the runtime's read envelope:\n265\t *   { workspace, subdir: \"views\", filename, relative_path, content, bytes }\n266\t *\n267\t * `content` is the raw UTF-8 string the file contains. By default the helper\n268\t * also parses it as JSON and exposes the result on the `view` property —\n269\t * pass `{ parseJson: false }` to skip parsing for non-JSON layouts (none\n270\t * exist today; the runtime guards JSON on save, but the read endpoint is\n271\t * format-agnostic).\n272\t *\n273\t * @param {string} workspace\n274\t * @param {string} filename\n275\t * @param {{ parseJson?: boolean }} [options]\n276\t */\n277\texport async function loadView(workspace, filename, options = {}) {\n278\t  const path = `/agent_sandbox/read/${encodeURIComponent(workspace)}/views/${encodeURIComponent(filename)}`;\n279\t  const response = await runtimeFetch(path);\n280\t  const envelope = await readRuntimeJson(response);\n281\t  const parseJson = options.parseJson !== false;\n282\t  if (parseJson && envelope && typeof envelope.content === \"string\") {\n283\t    try {\n284\t      envelope.view = JSON.parse(envelope.content);\n285\t    } catch (err) {\n286\t      const error = new Error(`loadView: stored content is not valid JSON: ${err.message}`);\n287\t      error.name = \"RuntimeError\";\n288\t      error.cause = err;\n289\t      throw error;\n290\t    }\n291\t  }\n292\t  return envelope;\n293\t}\n294\t\n295\t/**\n296\t * Phase D3 — list saved view filenames for a workspace.\n297\t *\n298\t * Returns the entries array directly (the runtime envelope's `entries` field).\n299\t * Pass `{ raw: true }` to get the full envelope `{ workspace, subdir, entries }`\n300\t * instead.\n301\t *\n302\t * @param {string} workspace\n303\t * @param {{ raw?: boolean }} [options]\n304\t */\n305\texport async function listViews(workspace, options = {}) {\n306\t  const path = `/agent_sandbox/list/${encodeURIComponent(workspace)}/views`;\n307\t  const response = await runtimeFetch(path);\n308\t  const envelope = await readRuntimeJson(response);\n309\t  if (options.raw) {\n310\t    return envelope;\n311\t  }\n312\t  return (envelope && Array.isArray(envelope.entries)) ? envelope.entries : [];\n313\t}\n314\t\n315\t/**\n316\t * Phase F — sign a `.aamp.view` layout via the runtime's HMAC chokepoint.\n317\t *\n318\t * Posts the view to `/api/views/sign` (intentionally outside the agent_sandbox\n319\t * prefix). `AgentScopeMiddleware` blocks any agent-scoped POST here with HTTP\n320\t * 403, so calling `signView` from a bound agent client surfaces the security\n321\t * boundary as a `RuntimeError(status=403)` — the runtime is the enforcer, not\n322\t * this module.\n323\t *\n324\t * Accepts a JSON-serialisable view object. The runtime computes HMAC-SHA256\n325\t * over a canonical payload (sorted keys, no whitespace, `signature` field\n326\t * stripped) and returns:\n327\t *\n328\t *   { signature: { algorithm, value, signed_at }, canonical_payload: \"...\" }\n329\t *\n330\t * The canonical payload is returned so callers can audit exactly what was