{"parentUuid":"64aea8d2-f6cc-4624-82e7-8741cc7ef91d","isSidechain":false,"attachment":{"type":"edited_text_file","filename":"C:\\Users\\nsdha\\OneDrive\\binary16\\prime-silo\\architecture\\ADR-003-agent-scope-credential-binding.md","snippet":"1\t# ADR-003 — Credential-bound agent scope (confused-deputy fix)\n2\t\n3\t- **Status:** Accepted (partial — see Residual Gap)\n4\t- **Date:** 2026-06-24\n5\t- **Supersedes the enforcement mechanism of:** [ADR-001](../runtime/architecture/ADR-001-prime-silo-shell-fork.md) determinism boundary\n6\t- **Related code:** `server/lib/runtime_proxy.js`, `server/lib/service_proxy.js`, `server/router/router.js`, `runtime/benny/api/agent_scope.py`, `app/L0/_all/mod/_prime_silo/runtime_client/runtime-client.js`\n7\t\n8\t## Context\n9\t\n10\tADR-001 introduced a determinism boundary: the in-browser agent runtime has full\n11\t_read_ access to Benny but its _writes_ are confined to `/api/agent_sandbox/`.\n12\tThat boundary was enforced entirely by a client-set header,\n13\t`X-Benny-Agent-Scope`, that the browser's agent runtime _voluntarily_ attached.\n14\t\n15\tA code review flagged this as a classic **confused-deputy / privilege\n16\tescalation** vulnerability:\n17\t\n18\t- The space-agent shell proxy injects the trusted `X-Benny-API-Key` on every\n19\t  `/api/runtime/*` call, so the request reaches Benny fully trusted.\n20\t- The proxy then **forwarded the client's `X-Benny-Agent-Scope` unchanged**.\n21\t- Therefore any authenticated caller (or agent-generated code) could simply\n22\t  **omit or alter** the scope header and reach Benny with full, unscoped trust —\n23\t  executing arbitrary swarm/graph/config mutations the sandbox was meant to deny.\n24\t\n25\tTwo further facts sharpened the problem:\n26\t\n27\t1. There is no agent-vs-human identity in the auth layer (`request_context.js`\n28\t   has no role/scope field), so the proxy cannot \"derive scope from RBAC.\"\n29\t2. Benny's `GovernanceMiddleware` is currently disabled (`server.py`), so the\n30\t   API key is not even validated — the scope header was the _only_ gate.\n31\t\n32\t## Decision\n33\t\n34\t**Bind scope to a server-injected credential, never to a client header.**\n35\t\n36\t### Two proxy facades (`server/lib/runtime_proxy.js`)\n37\t\n38\t| Facade | Path                   | Injects                         | Scope                                                                   |\n39\t| ------ | ---------------------- | ------------------------------- | ----------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n40\t| Human  | `/api/runtime/*`       | `BENNY_API_KEY` (trusted)       | client `X-Benny-Agent-Scope` **stripped** → normal RBAC                 |\n41\t| Agent  | `/api/agent-runtime/*` | `BENNY_AGENT_API_KEY` (sandbox) | `X-Benny-Agent-Scope: sandbox` **forced**, overwriting any client value |\n42\t\n43\tHeader filtering and stream piping are centralised in\n44\t`server/lib/service_proxy.js` so the \"drop these client headers, set these\n45\tserver headers\" decision lives in exactly one place.\n46\t\n47\t### Server-side enforcement (`runtime/benny/api/agent_scope.py`)\n48\t\n49\t`AgentScopeMiddleware` now computes an **effective scope** = the _most\n50\trestrictive_ of (a) the scope implied by the authenticating key — the sandbox\n51\tagent key pins the request to `sandbox` — and (b) the header scope. A caller can\n52\tonly **narrow** its confinement (`sandbox → read_only`), never widen it. The\n53\tboundary therefore holds even if the header is forged or the proxy is\n54\tmisconfigured.\n55\t\n56\t### Client routing (`runtime-client.js`)\n57\t\n58\tThe browser runtime client routes to the agent facade automatically whenever an\n59\tagent scope is active (`withAgentScope` / `fetchAsAgent` / a bound client) and to\n60\tthe human facade otherwise. The scope header is still sent for audit/back-compat,\n61\tbut the proxy is authoritative.\n62\t\n63\t### Credential hygiene\n64\t\n65\tThe hardcoded `DEFAULT_BENNY_API_KEY = \"benny-mesh-2026-auth\"` fallback is gone.\n66\tKeys resolve from env with dev-only fallbacks; in production\n67\t(`NODE_ENV=production`) `assertRuntimeProxyConfig()` throws at startup if\n68\t`BENNY_API_KEY` or `BENNY_AGENT_API_KEY` is missing. Set both to the same secret\n69\tin the Benny process and the shell process.\n70\t\n71\t## Residual Gap (why status is \"partial\")\n72\t\n73\tBoth facades are **same-origin**. In-page JavaScript — including agent-generated\n74\tcode — can still _choose_ to call `/api/runtime/*` (the human path) instead of\n75\t`/api/agent-runtime/*`. The credential binding makes scope unforgeable _given a\n76\tpath_, but it does not stop code from selecting the privileged path.\n77\t\n78\tMaking the boundary truly unbypassable requires the agent runtime to execute\n79\t**isolated from human JS** — e.g. a sandboxed Web Worker or cross-origin iframe\n80\twhose only channel to Benny is a broker that can reach the agent facade alone,\n81\twith CSP denying direct `/api/runtime` access. That is a larger change to the\n82\tspace-agent trust model and is tracked as the ADR-001 follow-up.\n83\t\n84\tUntil then, this ADR delivers: (1) scope can no longer be forged by tampering\n85\twith a header, (2) human and agent traffic carry distinct, scoped credentials,\n86\tand (3) no shipped default key in production.\n87\t\n88\t## Consequences\n89\t\n90\t- `tests/runtime_proxy_test.mjs` updated: the human facade now _strips_ the\n91\t  client scope; a new test asserts the agent facade forces sandbox + the agent\n92\t  key.\n93\t- Existing `runtime/tests/api/test_agent_sandbox.py` behaviour is preserved\n94\t  (those tests never send the agent key, so header-based behaviour is unchanged).\n95\t- Deployments must provision two secrets; see `docker-compose.yml` / DEVOPS.md."},"type":"attachment","uuid":"a5812db4-1782-40f3-aa69-2d924b8e984b","timestamp":"2026-06-24T14:40:37.712Z","userType":"external","entrypoint":"claude-desktop","cwd":"C:\\Users\